# Line endings and binary safety.
#
# Order matters: the LAST matching pattern wins, so the catch-all comes first
# and the binary declarations below override it.

# Everything git decides is text is stored and checked out as LF, on every
# platform. Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in its SYSTEM config,
# where it is invisible to `git config --global --list`, so without this the
# working tree drifts to CRLF while the blobs stay LF and nobody notices.
#
# Measured before this landed, at byte level: 1,114 of 1,116 tracked files were
# already LF in HEAD, but 566 were CRLF in the WORKING TREE. Git stayed quiet
# about the mismatch only because its stat cache matched and it never re-read
# the contents; editing any one of them made the whole file diff, which is
# exactly what happened to src/background.h on 2026-09-07.
#
# `text=auto` lets git decide what is text, and its rule is a NUL byte in the
# first 8000, so anything genuinely binary is left alone even if it is not
# named below.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary, declared explicitly rather than left to the heuristic. A raw RGB565
# raster is full of 0x0D and 0x0A bytes that mean pixel data, and a CRLF
# rewrite would both corrupt the image and desync the exact 411,640-byte size
# src/background.cpp checks before loading it.
#
# `binary` is shorthand for `-diff -merge -text`: never converted, never
# line-diffed, never auto-merged.
#
# Every binary extension actually present in the tree is listed. Verified by
# scanning all 1,116 tracked blobs for a NUL byte, not by guessing: .stl, .pdf,
# .gif and .mp3 came from that scan and would otherwise have been covered only
# by the heuristic.
*.rgb565 binary
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.bmp    binary
*.ttf    binary
*.bin    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.mp3    binary
*.stl    binary
